{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310690,"nonce":"C7Np7aSxTbuCwJvReOzBqj0IMFk=","title":"FIXED 884dfaf9: cross-module call targets bound through the symbol/constructor maps carried no name, so a stale id could never be rebound — ...","message":"**MEASURED 2026-08-18. Deterministic, seconds to reproduce, and it has been\nsilently costing nue a large multiple of decode throughput.**\n\n**THE REPRO — no model needed, traps print at COMPILE time.** From\n`nue/examples/llama-chat`, pass a nonexistent gguf so it fails right after\ncompiling:\n\n    rm -rf .rayzor\n    rayzor run Main.hx --preset server --release --llvm -- /nonexistent.gguf \"hi\" 4 512 0.5\n\nCount `W0020` lines. **Restore ⇒ traps; `--no-cache` ⇒ zero.** Before the\nplugin-discriminator fix the split was cold=0 / warm=3; after it (the snapshot\nnow hits on run 1) it is 3 either way. The trigger is RESTORE, not warmth.\n\n**THE CONSEQUENCE IS THE TIER, NOT JUST THE STUB.** A trap stub in any function\nmakes the bulk LLVM upgrade fail, and the whole process continues on Cranelift:\n\n    [tier] LLVM upgrade failed: ... IrId(100) not found in LLVM value_map\n           (continuing on Cranelift)\n\nInterleaved A/B, Qwen2.5-7B Q4_K_M, same busy box, alternating:\n\n| mode | tok/s | tier |\n|---|---|---|\n| cached | 4.55, 3.12 | **CRANELIFT** |\n| `--no-cache` | 6.79, 15.38 | LLVM |\n\nThe tier assignment is deterministic even though the box was noisy. **This is\nvery likely the origin of the \"Mistral 1.78–2.47 tok/s\" figure** — a cached run\non Cranelift. Mistral's own history file records 17–21 tok/s decode, and Qwen 7B\nmeasures 19.2 on a quiet box, so there was never a 6x model anomaly to explain.\n**Before benchmarking ANY nue change, confirm the run kept the LLVM tier** —\ngrep the output for `LLVM upgrade failed`. An A/B across that boundary is\nmeasuring the tier, not the change.\n\n**TWO DISTINCT CORRUPTIONS, both only on the restore path:**\n\n1. `Condition value IrId(N) not found` — a branch's SSA condition has no\n   defining instruction (`LlamaArch.build` IrId(100), `buildLinear` IrId(79)).\n2. `Called function IrFunctionId(N) not found in module` — a cross-module\n   callee dangles (`Conversation.buildSampler` → `LocalTempSampler.new`;\n   `WordPieceTokenizer.wordPiece` → 330008).\n\n**RULED OUT — do not re-test:**\n- **Optimization passes.** Disabling ALL of them\n  (`RAYZOR_DISABLE_PASSES=dead-code-elimination,constant-folding,copy-propagation,unreachable-block-elimination,control-flow-simplification,licm,cse,global_load_cache,gvn,tail-call-optimization,strip-stack-trace-updates,inlining,scalar_replacement,devirtualization`)\n  leaves all 3 traps. The corruption is in restore/merge/renumber, upstream of opt.\n- **The `.blade` serializer.** It is postcard+serde over the whole `IrModule`,\n  so there is no match arm to miss; `compiler/src/ir/blade.rs` has zero\n  `IrInstruction::` references.\n- **A generic cross-module `new`.** A minimal two-module repro (Factory.build →\n  `new Widget(n)` behind a ternary) is CLEAN cold, warm and warm2. Do not go\n  looking for a small syntactic trigger; it is specific to the restore of a\n  large module graph.\n\n**WHERE TO LOOK:** `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` (compilation.rs ~4650) exists\nfor exactly symptom 2 and is leaking — its error text literally says \"stale call\ntarget leaked past fixup_stale_cross_module_refs\". It is also full of BARE-NAME\nmatching (`real_funcs_by_bare_name`), against [[feedback_fqn_first_resolution]].\nSymptom 1 is NOT a cross-module problem at all and that pass cannot fix it.\n\nRelated: [[bugs_stdlib_artifacts_are_identical_restore_is_not]] already\nconcluded \"restoring a module is not equivalent to compiling it\"; this is the\nmeasured cost of that, and it is much larger than assumed.\n\n**NARROWED 2026-08-18 at `720b79dc` (after the plugin-discriminator fix).** The\n`Condition value` symptom is GONE; all remaining traps have ONE cause:\n\n    nue.tokenizer.WordPieceTokenizer.{wordPiece,encodeUtf8,decode}\n    reason: Called function IrFunctionId(330008) not found in module\n    CallDirect { dest: None, func_id: 330008, args: [IrId], arg_ownership: [Move] }\n\nThat call site is `out.push(id)` — **`Array.push`**. One arg, void, moved.\n\n**THE TARGET DOES NOT EXIST.** `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1` lists no `[fn-ptr]`\nanywhere in the 33xxxx range. Import ids are `100_000 + index*10_000`, so 330008\nis module #23 local id 8, and module #23 installs nothing. Bracketing by the\nbases that DO appear — 320000 `nue.tokenizer.MergeRule`, 350000\n`haxe.iterators.ArrayIterator` — puts #23 at the `Array` module, restored from\nthe snapshot and emitting no MIR function because `Array` dispatches through the\nstdlib mapping to a runtime symbol.\n\n**SO THE SHAPE IS:** a fresh module's call to a mapping-dispatched stdlib method\nis bound to a MIR function id from a restored module that never materialises one,\ninstead of being dispatched through the mapping. The earlier\n`[trap-stub:generic] IrFunctionId(420004) add (qn=\"StringBuf.add\")` has the same\nshape — `StringBuf` installs `grow` but not `add`.\n\n**NEXT:** find why `push`/`add` bind to a MIR id rather than a mapping entry when\nthe owning module is RESTORED (they bind correctly when it is compiled). Start at\n`renumber_and_push_import_mir` (compilation.rs ~5216) — it rewrites a CallDirect\nvia `external_function_names` → `stdlib_function_name_map`, else via the module's\nown `id_map`, and its own comment warns the cached id \"happens to alias something\nin this module's old id space\". Check whether a restored module populates\n`external_function_names` at all.\n\n**THE GAP, LOCATED (2026-08-18).** `compiler/src/ir/mir/mod.rs` ~1010 is the ONLY\nplace `external_function_names` is populated during lowering. It walks every\nCallDirect/FunctionRef/MakeClosure whose target is not a function of this module\nand records a name for it — **but only if `context.external_function_name_map`\nalready holds that id**. No entry there ⇒ the id is stored with NO NAME.\n\nThat is fatal downstream, because both later repair paths key on the name:\n`renumber_and_push_import_mir` (compilation.rs ~5216) rewrites via\n`external_function_names` → `stdlib_function_name_map`, and\n`fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` does the same. A nameless stale id can be\nrewritten by neither, so it survives to codegen as a trap stub.\n\nArithmetic confirms the id was ALREADY 330008 at lowering time, not mis-mapped\nlater: WordPieceTokenizer sits at base 520000, so its own `id_map` would have\nproduced 520008. It was lowered against the `Array` module that had been loaded\nat base 330000, whose MIR functions are then dropped at the stdlib merge because\n`Array` dispatches through the mapping to a runtime symbol.\n\n**THE FIX TO TRY (untried):** make the lowering record a qualified name for\nmapping-dispatched stdlib methods, so `external_function_names` always carries\none and the EXISTING fixup can rebind it. That is also the FQN-shaped fix —\n`unique_bare_match` in the fixup currently guesses by bare name + signature,\nwhich cannot disambiguate `push`/`add`/`new` and is against\n[[feedback_fqn_first_resolution]]. Do NOT paper over it by widening the bare-name\nmatch.\n\n**VERIFY WITH:** cold llama-chat trap count must be 0 and the run must NOT print\n`LLVM upgrade failed`; then re-run the interleaved cached-vs-`--no-cache` A/B and\nexpect the tier column to read LLVM for both.\n\n\n**FIXED 2026-08-18 in `884dfaf9`.** Root cause: lowering\n(`compiler/src/ir/mir/mod.rs` ~1010) recorded `external_function_names` ONLY for\ntargets bound through the name-keyed map. Targets bound through the SYMBOL-keyed\nmap or the CONSTRUCTOR map were stored with no name — and both repair passes\n(`renumber_and_push_import_mir`, `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs`) rebind BY NAME,\nso a nameless stale id was unrepairable and reached codegen as a trap stub.\n\n**The nameless one here was a CONSTRUCTOR, not `Array.push`** — the earlier\ninference from the instruction shape was wrong. `CallDirect { dest: None, args:\n[one], ownership: [Move] }` is ALSO the constructor ABI (`Ctor(this_ptr)`). The\n`ctor=true` probe settled it. Do not infer a callee from arity+void alone.\n\nFix: name them from their DECLARATION — the symbol's own `qualified_name`, or\n`format!(\"{class}.new\", ..)` from `context.constructor_name_map` (note: the\n`external_constructor_name_map` parameter is MOVED into the context before that\npoint, so read it from the context).\n\n**RESULT, interleaved on one box:** cached 4.55/3.12 tok/s on CRANELIFT →\n**15.5/16.1 on LLVM**, level with `--no-cache` (16.3/18.4). Traps 3 → 0.\n184/184 haxe tests, clippy clean.\n\n`RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1` now prints `[nameless-ext] \u003cid\u003e referenced by \u003cfn\u003e` if\nthis ever recurs — that line means a call site cannot be repaired, so treat it\nas a bug rather than noise.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_restored_modules_lose_the_llvm_tier` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}